-<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: MPL-2.0 -->
-
-# Kernelet userspace
-
-This workspace contains:
-the `/dev/kernelet` ABI,
-the OCI runtime,
-the guest PID 1 agent,
-and the containerd shim v2.
-The image ABI shared with the Host kernel —
-the ELF image format, its build contract,
-and where the loader and audit code live —
-is documented in [`ABI.md`](ABI.md).
-
-## Kernel build modes
-
-`kernelet-host` enables Host support and is disabled by default.
-The separate `kernelet` feature builds the Image; the two features are mutually
-exclusive and currently support only x86-64.
-Run these commands from `kernel/` inside the development container:
-
-| Command | Result |
-|---|---|
-| `cargo osdk build --release` | Native kernel without Kernelets Host support. |
-| `cargo osdk build --release --features kernelet-host` | Host support without an embedded Image. |
-| `cargo osdk build --release --kernelet` | Audited Image followed by a Host with `kernelet-host`, embedding that Image. |
-
-The paired build enables `kernelet` only for its separate Image invocation.
-Host builds with support enabled use panic unwinding for protected service
-continuations.
-Host builds use existing Fair scheduling weights without separate cumulative
-instance CPU accounting or hard CPU quotas. No additional scheduling feature
-is required. Explicit CPU measurements are available only in Image,
-experiment, diagnostic, or kernel-test builds.
-An explicitly enabled Host without an embedded Image does not register
-`/dev/kernelet`.
-
-Without Host support, production builds exclude carrier context switch
-hooks, Host timer services, NAT charge fields, carrier page-table ownership,
-and synchronous mapping-reclamation extensions.
-Explicit RQ1 fixture and diagnostic builds retain their measurement code;
-they are separate from production Native builds.
-Enabling Host support still changes shared paths even before creating an
-instance. This configuration needs its own performance measurements.
-
-The accepted scope of Host preemption checks and its relationship to the
-design book are recorded in
-[`Host preemption boundaries`](decisions/0001-host-preemption-boundaries.md).
-The selected scope of CPU register accessor protection and its prerequisites
-are recorded in [`CpuSync preemption`](decisions/0002-cpu-sync-preemption.md).
-The transitional Host scheduling design is described in
-[`Weight-only Host scheduling`](decisions/0003-weight-only-scheduling.md).
-Carrier identity cleanup is scoped to carrier transitions and fixed exit;
-see [`Carrier context boundaries`](decisions/0004-carrier-context-boundaries.md).
-Native address-space cache hits avoid hardware root checks by clearing the
-native cache at carrier entry; see
-[`Native VmSpace fast path`](decisions/0005-native-vmspace-fastpath.md).
-Carrier idle timeouts, exit waits, and reaper retries use the existing Host
-monotonic timers; see [`Host native timers`](decisions/0006-host-native-timers.md).
-Ordinary Host trap returns skip Image processing at the private carrier-pointer
-check; see [`Carrier trap fast path`](decisions/0008-carrier-trap-fastpath.md).
-Images read time locally from immutable TSC calibration, without a periodically
-published clock page; see [`Local TSC clock`](decisions/0007-local-tsc-clock.md).
-Carrier FPU preservation remains enabled; possible overlap with inner thread
-saves awaits measurement. See
-[`Carrier FPU preservation`](decisions/0009-carrier-fpu-preservation.md).
-Synchronous carrier root activation switches directly to the registered target,
-retaining the old reservation through CR3 commit; see
-[`Carrier direct root activation`](decisions/0010-carrier-direct-root-activation.md).
-Host queues per-vCPU execution samples; Image settles their Task ownership
-at upcalls and logical context handoffs. See
-[`Image pending CPU accounting`](decisions/0011-image-pending-cpu-accounting.md).
-Host stack authentication uses fixed layout and atomic live-bit reads;
-allocation and release retain the allocator lock. See
-[`Stack live validation`](decisions/0012-stack-live-validation.md).
-
-## Userspace build
-
-Build all three statically linked programs with:
-
-```sh
-cargo build --manifest-path kernelet/Cargo.toml --release --target x86_64-unknown-linux-musl
-```
-
-For the Asterinas initramfs,
-pass the built runtime, agent, and shim as explicit inputs to `make initramfs`
-as described in
-[`test/initramfs/README.md`](../test/initramfs/README.md#kernelet-userspace).
-That image includes an OCI Bash bundle with SQLite
-and a Nix-packaged `mke2fs` with its runtime closure.
-
-The Host installation contains:
-
-- `kernelet-runtime` and `containerd-shim-kernelet-v2` on `PATH`;
-- `/usr/libexec/kernelet-agent`, the static guest binary;
-- `/usr/libexec/kernelet-mke2fs`, a static `mke2fs` supplied by the distribution.
-
-The OCI CLI accepts
-`create`, `start`, `state`, `stats`, `kill`, `delete`, `exec`, and `resize`.
-Global `--root`, `--cache`, `--agent`, and `--mke2fs` options
-select installation paths.
-`create --bundle <directory> <id>` consumes a standard OCI bundle.
-`--max-tasks` selects the internal Task limit, defaulting to 256 and capped at
-4096.
-The `org.asterinas.kernelet.max-tasks` OCI annotation overrides this value
-for both CLI and shim creation.
-This counts kernel Tasks, including background
-threads; it is separate from the unsupported OCI PID controller.
-
-Large syscall workloads can use a 2048 MiB OCI memory limit and a 4096 Task
-limit.
-Boot the Host with `kernelet_user_memory_mib=4096` to authorize a 4096 MiB
-per-user total reservation.
-The default remains 1024 MiB.
-Values must be positive multiples of 2 MiB.
-This total includes Guest memory ceilings and Host execution
-and metadata storage; raising the Image ceiling alone does not raise it.
-Host physical allocation can still fail within the configured reservation.
-
-`start --attach <id>` attaches the container to the calling terminal:
-a `terminal=true` process runs interactively on the Guest PTY
-and the CLI restores the tty when the attach ends,
-while a `terminal=false` process has its standard streams forwarded.
-`create --console-socket <socket>` remains for a manager,
-transferring the Host PTY master with `SCM_RIGHTS`.
-`exec --process <json> <id>` uses an OCI process object.
-`pause`, `resume`, and `update` return an unsupported-operation error.
-
-`stats <id>` reports JSON with
-CPU execution, throttling, completion and receive-copy CPU time,
-granted memory, memory limit, Host charges, and stacks.
-Cumulative CPU time is currently unavailable: stats and status JSON report
-`cpu_time_ns: null`, and containerd metrics omit CPU usage.
-The raw `STATS` and `STATUS` ioctls use `STATS_UNAVAILABLE` (`UINT64_MAX`) for
-uncollected CPU observations, preserving the structures' layouts.
-Completion and receive-copy CPU observations remain optional diagnostics;
-ordinary builds report them as `null`.
-Host creation and budget updates reject nonzero CPU quota with `EOPNOTSUPP`.
-Throttle time is zero. Memory, log limits and worker lifecycle ownership remain
-available. Carrier weights are divided by the fixed vCPU count; workers retain
-the Host's default Fair weight. This is not complete instance group scheduling.
-
-The runtime applies these OCI resource fields at creation:
-
-| Field | Current behavior |
-|---|---|
-| `linux.resources.memory.limit` | A nonnegative limit is rounded down to a whole 2 MiB grain, with creation failing below one grain; a negative or omitted limit uses `--max-grains`. |
-| `linux.resources.cpu.cpus` | The number of distinct CPU numbers selects the vCPU count; the Host's placement policy selects Host CPUs independently of those numbers. |
-| `linux.resources.cpu.shares` | A positive value maps to the rounded nice value `10 - log2(shares)`, clamped to `[-20, 19]`; its fair weight is divided among the configured carriers. |
-| `linux.resources.cpu.quota` and `period` | A positive quota is unsupported and rejected with `EOPNOTSUPP`; a nonpositive or omitted quota requests no hard bandwidth limit. |
-| `linux.resources.pids.limit` | A nonnegative limit is rejected; a negative limit does not install a finite OCI PID limit. |
-
-For `N` configured vCPUs, each carrier receives `W/N` of the fair weight
-selected by the sandbox's nice value.
-The scheduler retains fractional precision instead of rounding to another nice value.
-Later CPU budget updates preserve this divisor.
-This normalizes the carriers' total weight;
-it does not implement a sandbox group scheduler.
-Sleeping carriers do not transfer their weight to the remaining runnable carriers,
-and CPU placement still affects the time each carrier receives.
-Adopted device workers remain separate Host scheduling entities outside this split.
-
-`--max-grains` defaults to 256 grains (512 MiB) of Guest memory.
-The Host's default per-user admission budget is 512 grains (1 GiB) in total,
-including Guest memory ceilings and reserved Host execution storage.
-The runtime default leaves room for Host Task stacks and carrier resources.
-An explicit OCI memory limit or `--max-grains` value still sets the Guest
-ceiling; creation fails if that ceiling plus Host reservations exceeds
-the remaining per-user budget.
-
-Other resource fields,
-including memory reservation and swap limits,
-block I/O limits,
-huge-page limits,
-and CPU memory-node selection,
-are currently accepted without being applied.
-
-The runtime builds content-addressed ext2 images,
-including the agent in the root image.
-The cached images are attached read-only.
-The agent mounts a tmpfs upper and an overlay root
-before processing the configuration.
-Bind files are sent separately through the agent protocol
-and preserve ownership and mode;
-read-only directory binds become separate cached block images.
-The runtime and agent require protocol version `4` in `Hello`;
-build and deploy them together.
-This version carries acknowledged `SignalAll` requests for every Guest process
-except the PID 1 agent, including descendants that changed session or process group.
-Per-process signals still address the selected launcher's process group.
-Signal `0` probes existence without terminating a created process or sandbox.
-A rootfs or agent that changes during image assembly
-fails creation
-instead of publishing a cache entry under an incorrect content key.
-
-The CLI starts a holder after its own `exec`
-and transfers its descriptors with an acknowledgment.
-Later CLI invocations reconnect to its Unix control socket.
-The containerd shim initializes that same holder in its own process
-and retains the sandbox descriptors itself.
-Containerd reconnects through the shim `Connect` and `State` RPCs.
-If a holder dies,
-its last capability closes and the sandbox is revoked;
-`state` reports stopped,
-and `delete` performs remaining host cleanup.
-The shim persists the observed main-process exit
-for its crash cleanup command.
-If no exit was observed,
-crash cleanup reports forced termination (137).
-During normal deletion, `poststop` hooks can call `State` and `Wait`;
-the task remains registered until cleanup finishes.
-
-The agent gives stdout and stderr up to one second to finish after obtaining
-the process's exit status.
-The shim forwards each output stream through its own worker,
-with a queue of at most eight 16 KiB chunks,
-and uses nonblocking writes to avoid waiting indefinitely for a FIFO consumer.
-It also bounds queue handoff and output draining after exit to one second.
-Once it receives the exit status, it stops attachment input forwarding
-before waiting for output workers.
-A stream that cannot drain reports a local output failure and may be truncated;
-the process keeps its actual exit code.
-If an attachment disconnects,
-the shim queries the holder's exit cache through a separate control connection.
-An unavailable exit status makes `Wait` return an error.
-Descendants that leave their launcher's process group can remain alive after
-that process exits;
-`kill(all=true)` signals these descendants as well.
-Sandbox destruction terminates the remaining Guest execution.
-
-Shim `Stats` exposes granted/capped memory and zero throttle time through
-containerd's cgroups metrics format. CPU usage is omitted while cumulative
-instance CPU accounting is unavailable.
-Granted memory is an allocation, not Guest RSS.
-`Pids` queries the agent
-and returns PIDs in the Guest PID namespace;
-these are not Host PIDs and exclude the agent itself.
-
-## Networking
-
-Networking is selected through these annotations
-(the names are runtime API choices, not kernel ABI constants):
-
-- `org.asterinas.kernelet.network`: `none` (default) or `nat`.
-- `org.asterinas.kernelet.ports`:
-  a JSON array of objects with `hostPort`, `containerPort`,
-  optional `hostAddress` (default `127.0.0.1`),
-  and `protocol` (`tcp` or `udp`).
-
-Each sandbox uses `10.0.2.15/24`, gateway `10.0.2.2`, and DNS proxy `10.0.2.3`.
-The DNS proxy reaches the Host's configured IPv4 resolver,
-including a resolver bound to the Host loopback address.
-These addresses belong to each sandbox's separate Ethernet link.
-
-The Guest uses the kernel's static Ethernet configuration with either device backend.
-The agent writes the DNS resolver file
-without changing the link, address, or route through rtnetlink.
-Custom Guest IPv4 addresses and gateways are not supported.
-
-The runtime performs network configuration only;
-the Host kernel forwards all payload traffic.
-After creating the sandbox's network endpoint,
-the runtime issues one `NET_CONFIG` ioctl on the endpoint file
-carrying the Host resolver and up to 32 port mappings,
-then attaches the endpoint to the sandbox with the guest MAC.
-The Host validates the configuration
-and binds the mapped host ports synchronously during `NET_CONFIG`,
-so `create` fails before acknowledging a container
-whose ports are already occupied.
-After the attach,
-the runtime keeps no network descriptor and exchanges no packets.
-The existing per-user endpoint memory quota
-also applies to listeners and active forwarding flows.
-ICMP forwarding and IPv6 are not provided.
-
-## Device selection for ordinary applications
-
-The `org.asterinas.kernelet.io-devices` OCI annotation selects block and network
-backends before the sandbox starts.
-Its value is a JSON object encoded as a string:
-
-```json
-{
-  "annotations": {
-    "org.asterinas.kernelet.network": "nat",
-    "org.asterinas.kernelet.io-devices": "{\"block\":\"borrowed\",\"network\":\"shared\",\"blockBackings\":[\"/dev/vdb\"]}"
-  }
-}
-```
-
-`block` accepts `copy` (default) or `borrowed`.
-`network` accepts `copy` (default) or `shared`; shared mode requires enabled networking.
-The choices are independent.
-Omitting the annotation preserves the existing copying devices.
-Native builds retain their existing I/O paths.
-Applications continue using ordinary file, `mmap`, TCP, and UDP calls with the same Linux ABI.
-
-Borrowed block mode uses `BORROWED_BLOCK` with the existing Image VirtIO block driver.
-The Host submits BIOs over the Image's registered DMA pages and retains their leases until physical I/O ends.
-It does not allocate a Host staging payload buffer or copy payload bytes across this boundary.
-The backend currently supports coherent x86-64, non-confidential VMs, disabled IOMMU DMA remapping,
-and opened whole-device VirtIO block backings.
-Regular files, partitions, other drivers, and unsupported DMA environments fail attachment with `EOPNOTSUPP`.
-There is no copying fallback for a requested borrowed device.
-
-Borrowed mode requires explicit absolute `blockBackings` paths.
-The array supplies the root backing first, then directory-bind image backings in OCI mount order.
-Its length must match the generated root and bind-image count.
-Prepare the corresponding filesystems in advance, including the agent in the root backing.
-The runtime does not copy its generated filesystem images onto these devices.
-The runtime retains its read-only backing and writable tmpfs-overlay policy in both block modes.
-Control clients can also attach a writable `BORROWED_BLOCK` directly before `START`.
-
-Shared network mode attaches `SHARED_NET` to the configured Host NAT endpoint.
-The Image's shared Ethernet device implements the ordinary network-device interface used by `aster-bigtcp`.
-It passes owning packet storage across the Host/Image boundary without staging payload copies.
-Bounded buffer pools, import credit, and return notifications recycle allocations.
-Shared queues and NAT receive tokens carry compact allocation handles.
-The copying device keeps its inline Ethernet frames.
-The worker retains one prepared shared transmit buffer when a paired receive token leaves it unused.
-Worker-local progress does not notify the worker again; external handoffs and cancellation still wake it.
-The Image exchanges up to 32 empty returns and 32 received grants in one private device call.
-This call combines receipt with activation and leaves rejected return buffers with their original owner.
-Host forwarding and socket configuration remain in the existing kernel network path.
-TCP stream storage, application buffers, native Host sockets, and physical NICs can still involve copies;
-this device does not establish zero-copy operation throughout TCP/IP.
-
-`Sandbox::io_path_stats` and the `IO_PATH_STATS` ioctl expose borrowed block activity,
-original DMA address checks, shared network traffic, allocation reuse, and compatibility-copy counters.
-They describe the selected device boundaries, not all copies in the system.
-
-## Explicit zero-copy block attachments
-
-Native I/O and existing `BLOCK` attachments keep their existing paths.
-An additional `ZERO_COPY_BLOCK` attachment exposes the shared-buffer interface.
-Its indices are independent of VirtIO MMIO device IDs and Linux disk names.
-Before `Sandbox::start`, a control client can call:
-
-```rust,ignore
-let index = sandbox.attach_zero_copy_block(&backing, true)?;
-```
-
-The backing must be an opened whole-device VirtIO block device.
-Buffered files, partitions, and unsupported drivers fail with `EOPNOTSUPP`.
-The ordinary `BLOCK` interface remains available for those backends.
-Zero-copy attachments do not replace the root filesystem's ordinary block device.
-
-Image kernel clients use `ostd::kernelet::shared_buffer::ZeroCopyBlock::open(index)`.
-Its `read`, `write`, and `write_read_only` methods consume shared buffer wrappers.
-Rejection returns the original wrapper; accepted work returns a fresh grant through `PendingIo::poll`.
-Cancellation retains storage until backend access ends.
-These devices use no guest descriptor ring or staging payload buffer.
-The OCI runtime accepts explicit attachments through an annotation containing a JSON array:
-
-```json
-"org.asterinas.kernelet.zero-copy-blocks": "[{\"path\":\"/dev/vdb\",\"readOnly\":true}]"
-```
-
-Array order determines zero-copy device indices.
-The annotation defaults to no attachments and does not change the root disk or copying devices.
-
-See [the implementation contract](decisions/0014-shared-buffers-and-service-stacks.md)
-for ownership, accounting, stack handling, and validation limits.
-
-## Shared-buffer datagram devices
-
-Authorize a connection between two running instances through their private holder sockets:
-
-```sh
-kernelet-runtime --root /run/kernelet shared-buffer-connect producer consumer
-```
-
-The command holds capabilities for both sandboxes and prints their connection identities.
-Repeating it returns the existing connection.
-The raw `SHARED_BUFFER_CONNECT` ioctl and `Sandbox::connect_shared_buffers` expose the same operation.
-
-Each connection exposes an independent `ZeroCopyNet` device in both images.
-Image kernel clients open it with `ZeroCopyNet::open(index)` and create an owning-buffer datagram socket with `socket()`.
-Producers allocate shared storage, write directly through its exclusive writer, and consume it through `try_send`.
-Consumers obtain `ZeroCopyDatagram` through `try_recv` and parse the original allocation through `reader()`.
-The kernel socket adapter also supports importing and forwarding an existing grant without copying payload bytes.
-
-This interface supports datagrams of 0 to 65535 bytes and nonblocking backpressure.
-It provides no Ethernet, IP, or UDP framing and does not select a physical network interface.
-These connections are independent of the startup-selected Ethernet device and Host NAT endpoint.
-Ordinary Linux socket traffic uses the selected copying or shared Ethernet path.
-Accepted datagrams survive sender exit; the receiver can discover and drain them before the disconnected device disappears.
-
-## Shared I/O functional regression
-
-Inside the development container, install this checkout's OSDK and run:
-
-```sh
-OSDK_LOCAL_DEV=1 cargo install --path osdk --locked
-python3 tools/test_kernelet_shared_io.py all
-```
-
-The test creates dedicated images under `target/kernelet-shared-io`.
-It boots a paired Host/Image build in QEMU/KVM with a dedicated VirtIO block disk.
-Two images exercise the real buffer and socket APIs, validate block readback, and compare allocation addresses across datagram round trips.
-The temporary procfs test entry exists only in builds with `KERNELET_SHARED_IO_TEST=1`.
-Results and complete build and VM logs remain in that directory.
-This functional test does not measure performance or establish physical NIC copy elimination.
-
-The transparent-device regression exercises ordinary file, mapping, TCP, and UDP calls:
-
-```sh
-python3 tools/test_kernelet_transparent_io.py all
-```
-
-It prepares separate copying and shared root disks under `target/kernelet-transparent-io`.
-Both Images read and write files, persist a `MAP_SHARED` modification, and exchange TCP/UDP data with native Host servers.
-The runner checks Image exit status, device counters, completed teardown, and both disks' contents offline.
-Results apply to the tested Host/Image pair; rerun the regression after the final source freeze.
-Complete results and logs remain in that test directory.
-No latency or throughput result is established by these fixtures.
-
-## C ABI header
-
-Generate a C header from the shared Rust definitions with:
-
-```sh
-cargo run --manifest-path kernelet/abi/Cargo.toml --example header -- kernelet/target/kernelet-abi.h
-cc -std=c11 -Werror -fsyntax-only -x c kernelet/target/kernelet-abi.h
-```
-
-The generator evaluates ioctl constants in Rust
-and emits C structure-size assertions;
-kernel and runtime consume the same `kernelet-abi` Rust crate.
-
-## Validation
-
-```sh
-cargo clippy --manifest-path kernelet/Cargo.toml --all-targets -- -D warnings
-KERNELET_TEST_MKE2FS=/path/to/mke2fs cargo test --manifest-path kernelet/Cargo.toml -- --include-ignored
-```
-
-Native tests cover
-message bounds, descriptor transfer, and actual ext2 image creation and reuse.
-They also exercise shim RPC deletion races
-and hook callbacks through `State` and `Wait`,
-plus local forwarding with real FIFOs and sockets under input backpressure.
-They do not prove that the full guest boot, overlay, OCI lifecycle,
-containerd integration, or Host network forwarding paths work.
-Those require the integrated QEMU tests
-and a Host kernel implementing the resource and lifecycle ABI.
